This is a modified fork of Liam ERD, pinned to upstream commit 92156eac5
(2026-06-18). Files that differ from that commit carry a notice at the top of
the file, as required by section 4(b) of the Apache License, Version 2.0.

Summary of the changes made to the original work
------------------------------------------------

1. Table positions are persisted.
   Upstream recomputes the automatic layout on every load, so any table the
   user dragged snapped back on refresh. Positions now resolve as
   URL (?positions=) > browser storage > layout.json > automatic layout, and a
   table that is not pinned still falls back to the automatic layout.

2. Canvas memos.
   Free-form notes can be pinned to the diagram, shipped with the build in
   memos.json and edited in the browser. A memo can be duplicated from the
   context menu or copied and pasted, including into another tab.

3. Colour coding.
   Tables and memos can be tinted from a fixed palette taken from the existing
   design tokens. A table's colour is stored alongside its position.

4. A read-only default with an explicit edit mode.
   Everything above is read-only unless the page is opened with ?edit=1, so a
   shared link cannot be rearranged by accident.

5. MySQL schema export.
   Upstream can deparse a schema to PostgreSQL and YAML only. A MySQL deparser
   was added, and the export menu can now copy the DDL to the clipboard or
   download it as a .sql file.

6. Multi-selection on the canvas.
   In edit mode a drag across empty canvas selects tables and memos, which can
   then be moved, tinted or deleted as a set. Upstream leaves the left button
   inert and acts on one table at a time. Upstream's global Cmd/Ctrl-C
   shortcut for copying the page link was removed so the key belongs to that
   selection; the command palette still offers "Copy Link".

7. [Retired] A Windows compatibility fix to an npm script in frontend/apps/app,
   which otherwise prevented the repository's own lint task from running.
   frontend/apps/app was itself removed as an unused upstream package — see
   item 12 — so this fix no longer applies to anything in the repository.

8. Table grouping.
   Tables can be gathered into human-authored, named groups, drawn as a
   dashed, tinted border box with a label on the canvas. A table may belong
   to more than one group. Groups are shipped with the build in groups.json,
   editable in the browser via the canvas context menu, and can be toggled
   off (single view) without losing the underlying data. The left sidebar
   optionally sections the table list by group to match.

9. The command-line tool is redistributed under a different name.
   Section 6 of the License grants no trademark rights, so the npm package,
   its executable, this repository, the generated page title, the browser
   storage keys and the browser console helpers no longer carry the "Liam"
   product name; they are published as "crowfoot". They were briefly published
   as "erdkit" (0.1.0 through 0.4.2) before settling on the present name. A
   value still held under a previous "liam:" or "erdkit:" storage key is moved
   to the current key the first time it is read, so nothing a user arranged is
   lost. The tool names Liam ERD as the work it is derived from in its banner
   and its --help output. No endorsement by ROUTE06, Inc. is implied.

10. The terminal banner no longer uses the upstream wordmark or its colours.
    The ASCII art spells the fork's own name, and it is drawn in a single
    colour of this fork's choosing rather than Liam ERD's brand green
    (#1DED83).

11. The viewer no longer presents itself as Liam ERD. Its heading, logo mark
    and favicon are this fork's own, and the links that asserted product
    identity — homepage, repository, release notes, community forum — now
    point at this fork or have been removed. Links to upstream documentation
    are kept where it remains accurate for this fork, and are labelled as
    upstream so they do not read as this project's own. The per-file notices
    required by section 4(b), the attribution in the banner and --help output,
    and this file are unaffected: what is dropped is trademark use under
    section 6, not attribution under section 4.

12. Unused upstream packages were removed. The fork's shipped CLI and viewer
    depend on only four packages (schema, erd-core, cli, ui); everything else
    inherited from upstream — the Next.js apps (frontend/apps/{app,assets,docs,
    erd-sample}), the inert internal-packages (agent, db, e2e,
    figma-to-css-variables, github, mcp-server, pglite-server, schema-bench,
    security, storybook), a package.json-less orphan (packages/db-structure)
    and dead Turbo generator scaffolding — was deleted. Root tooling (turbo.json,
    package.json, pnpm-workspace.yaml, knip.jsonc, .syncpackrc, vitest.config.ts)
    was updated to match. The per-file notices required by section 4(b) that
    lived in the packages the fork ships are unaffected; none of the 92 files
    carrying one lived in a deleted package.

13. In-browser schema editing.
    In edit mode the table detail panel becomes a form covering the whole of a
    table's definition — name, comment, columns, primary/foreign/unique/check
    constraints and indexes — and tables can be added, renamed and removed. A
    foreign key can also be drawn between two tables from the canvas context
    menu. Renames and deletions carry every reference that pointed at what
    changed. Edits live in ?schemaedits= and are folded into the schema every
    consumer reads, so the DDL export reflects them; nothing is written back to
    schema.json. Upstream's viewer is read-only.
